  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 150 AM Wednesday... \n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... While overcast skies are forecast today and \nThursday morning, conditions will remain dry.\n\nAn area of low clouds has developed across eastern North Carolina \nand the clouds will spread west overnight, reaching the Triad around \nsunrise. There could also be some patchy fog as well around sunrise. \nThe clouds will slowly rise in height during the day, but no rain is \nin the forecast. These clouds will continue overnight and into \nThursday, slowly eroding from east to west during the day. The \nclouds will keep today's highs a couple degrees cooler than \nyesterday, closer to normal for this time of year, and result in \nmild low temperatures tonight, generally in the 50s.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... Forecast uncertainty remains high on next chance \nfor measurable rainfall and amounts during the first week of \nNovember.\n\nModel guidance continue to struggle with the forecast pattern \nevolution through the first week of November with two primary \ndrivers in this uncertainty. The first is the moisture associated \nwith Tropical Cyclone Vance as it lifts through southwest Mexico and \ninto the Four Corners and Southern Plains region Fri before shifting \neastward into the Southeast Sat into Sun and perhaps Carolinas Sun \nnight into Mon. Locally over the Carolinas ahead of this influx of \ndeep-layer moisture, a reinforcing dry cold front will provide \nrenewed high pressure persisting over the Mid-Atlantic into the \nCarolinas this weekend and could provide a favorable setup for \nisentropic ascent and overrunning given adequate forcing/moisture in \nplace. However, outside of the GFS and its ensemble suite, most \nguidance is shunting the better QPF signal farther south with only \nlight accumulations in NC Sun night into Tues night.\n\nThe second and perhaps better signal may exist beyond the current \nforecast period as a strong mid/upper low shifts from the West Coast \non Sun into the Central Plains into the mid-week timeframe. How and \nwhen this feature will develop is understandably uncertain as the \ndriving features will not phase until this weekend. The general \nconsensus among ensemble guidance highlights the Wed/Thurs-night \nwindow for increased rain chances, but details are muddy at this \ntime.",
